@@ -335,6 +357,44 @@ Validators
 Backwards incompatible changes in 6.2
 =====================================
 
+.. _multiline-template-tags-compatibility:
+
+Multiline template tag parsing
+------------------------------
+
+Previously, ``{%`` and ``%}`` sequences on separate lines were treated as text.
+These may now be interpreted as a template tag. This can change the rendered
+output or raise a :exc:`~django.template.TemplateSyntaxError`. Use the
+:ttag:`templatetag` tag with ``openblock`` and ``closeblock`` to output these
+sequences as text.
+
+For example, previously the following was rendered as text:
+
+.. code-block:: text
+
+    This is the opening delimiter {%.
+    This is the closing delimiter %}.
+
+To preserve this output, use:
+
+.. code-block:: html+django
+
+    This is the opening delimiter {% templatetag openblock %}.
+    This is the closing delimiter {% templatetag closeblock %}.
+
+Whitespace within the names of named :ttag:`verbatim` tags is now treated as
+equivalent when matching closing tags. Previously, ``{% verbatim a   b %}``
+required ``{% endverbatim a   b %}`` to close it. It now also matches
+``{% endverbatim a b %}``, which may cause a verbatim block to end earlier than
+intended.
+
+You can temporarily restore the previous parsing behavior by setting
+``"allow_multiline_tags": False`` in the
+:class:`~django.template.backends.django.DjangoTemplates` backend's
+:setting:`OPTIONS <TEMPLATES-OPTIONS>`. Remove the option once your templates
+are updated. This transitional option is deprecated and will be removed in
+Django 2029.
+
 Database backend API
